    "notes": "Thresholds were mapped according to the screening model criteria: 'alert' corresponds to the national empirical 3-day cumulative threshold for 50% landslide probability (47.66 mm, Source 1 / inline citation 4); 'warning' corresponds to the JKR/JICA operational 1-day criterion (>100 mm/day, Source 2 / inline citation 5); and 'danger' corresponds to the MetMalaysia 1-day continuous heavy rain danger advisory (>240 mm/day, Source 3 / inline citation 6). The minimum landslide-prone slope angle of 25 degrees (Class 3 terrain requiring mandatory EIA and LVA) is supported by Malaysian hilly terrain guidelines (Source 6 / inline citations 1, 2, 3). Major caveats apply when transposing Peninsular Malaysian thresholds to the Kelantan River Basin: empirical studies are heavily biased toward the West Coast and Cameron Highlands, often rely on rain gauges 5 to 20 km from failure sites, and do not account for the wetter hydro-geomorphological baseline of the East Coast during the Northeast Monsoon (where 5-day antecedent rainfall and extreme multi-day deluges, such as the 1,295 mm over 3 days in December 2014, govern failure)."
